Chamfers and fillets: a gap over the start of a closed spine, cut by the
face beyond, split at the spine's start (mitcad#263).

An analytic blend along a closed edge whose contact on one face leaves that
face across an edge into a face beyond and comes back gets a piece cut by
the face beyond over each such gap (0055, 0071, 0142; on a closed spine the
gap from the last piece round to the first too, 0057, 0072, 0145). Where
that gap holds the spine's start (a post on a plate, its foot chamfered so
wide that the cone's contact on the plate runs past the plate's side where
the post's seam is), the piece ran across the start: its other contact
crossed the seam of the face closed round the spine (the post's cylinder,
whose edge's vertex is on the seam) inside the piece. The topological build
then took the contact's edges on that face for a wire of their own and kept
the face whole, with its old edge at the spine: an invalid shape.

- ChFi3d_SplitPieceAtSection (new): splits a piece of an analytic blend at
  the section through a spine point (the plane square to the spine there),
  both parts on the same blend surface (one face, as 0073's pieces), each
  line cut where it crosses that plane; where a line is cut on an edge of
  its face (the seam), both parts' points there are on that edge, on a seam
  on the side each part runs into the face from (as ChFi3d_KPartCutPiece's
  ends).
- ChFi3d_FillKPartGaps: on a closed spine, once the gaps are filled and the
  pieces' lines and p-curves taken into their periods, a piece holding the
  spine's start is split there where a line crosses the seam of its face
  there (a piece whose lines cross no seam there, e.g. a rounding's torus
  over a gap, stays whole: its face's seam stays where it was); its part
  before the start goes to the end of the period, the part after it to its
  start, and the lines and p-curves are taken into their periods again.

Upstream: new.
